Sorry.  I must have pasted things incorrectly.  I've attached a file with the original code and the output that I just ran.

And I'm apparently not making my point very well.  My original point is that including single-visit sites does influence the maximum likelihood estimate for the detection probability (as well as the estimate for the probability of occupancy).  The size of the influence depends on the "true" values and the number of single-visit and multiple-visit sites.  Again, the point is that the level of influence is not zero.

I certainly don't recommend such low levels sampling.  (And that is not to say that low levels of sampling are inexpensive.  Also, folks might not have any choice as one can inherit such designs.)  I think we all agree that one must assess how well one might estimate desired parameters (whether that be through simulations or some other approach).  Asymptotics don't always work so well.

What did surprise me with the simulations is that with the maximum likelihood estimators, standard errors and mean square errors could get larger despite an increase in sampling effort.  So a definite YES on determining what might be expected with any proposed sampling effort.

Jim

